1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,117
Book Four, Proposition Two.

2
00:00:02,117 --> 00:00:06,830
In a given circle to inscribe a triangle equiangular with a given triangle.

3
00:00:06,830 --> 00:00:16,570
Let A B C be the given circle, and D E F the given triangle; it is required to inscribe in the circle A B C a triangle equiangular with the triangle D E F.

4
00:00:16,990 --> 00:00:24,087
Draw the straight line G H touching the circle A B C at A by the Porism to Three Sixteen.

5
00:00:24,087 --> 00:00:37,085
On A H at the point A, construct the angle H A C equal to the angle D E F by One Twenty-Three; and on A G at A, construct the angle G A B equal to the angle D F E.

6
00:00:37,085 --> 00:00:38,600
Join the chord B C.

7
00:00:39,020 --> 00:00:54,138
Because A H touches the circle A B C and the chord A C is drawn across from the point of contact A, by Three Thirty-Two the tangent-chord angle H A C equals the angle A B C in the alternate segment of the circle.

8
00:00:54,138 --> 00:01:00,699
Since angle H A C was constructed equal to angle D E F, angle A B C also equals angle D E F.

9
00:01:00,699 --> 00:01:08,472
For the same reason, angle G A B equals the alternate-segment angle A C B, so angle A C B equals angle D F E.

10
00:01:08,472 --> 00:01:14,819
Therefore, by One Thirty-Two, the remaining angle B A C equals the remaining angle E D F.

11
00:01:14,819 --> 00:01:23,162
Thus in the given circle A B C there has been inscribed the triangle A B C equiangular with the given triangle D E F.

12
00:01:23,162 --> 00:01:24,660
Which was to be done.

13
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:33,888
Proposition Four Two converts the alternate-segment theorem of Book Three into an instrument for transferring any triangular form into any circle.

14
00:01:33,888 --> 00:01:44,505
Once a point of contact A is chosen, six congruent orientations of the inscribed triangle arise by permuting which vertex angle is placed at A and which way the other two face.

15
00:01:44,505 --> 00:01:56,208
As De Morgan noted, combined with One One, this construction immediately inscribes an equilateral triangle in any circle—a step Euclid assumes without separate proof in Proposition Four Sixteen.

16
